      <li><strong>Implementation &amp; runtime.</strong> <a href="https://github.com/astral-sh/ty" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ty</a>: &ldquo;An extremely fast Python type checker and language server, written in Rust.&rdquo; <a href="https://engineering.fb.com/2025/05/15/developer-tools/open-sourcing-pyrefly-a-faster-python-type-checker-written-in-rust/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Pyrefly</a>: Meta&rsquo;s announcement is titled &ldquo;Open-sourcing Pyrefly: A faster Python type checker written in Rust.&rdquo; <a href="https://github.com/zubanls/zuban" target="_blank" rel="noopener">zuban</a>: &ldquo;a high-performance Python Language Server and type checker implemented in Rust, by the author of Jedi.&rdquo; <a href="https://microsoft.github.io/pyright/#/installation" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Pyright</a> (TypeScript) states you must install &ldquo;node (which Pyright requires).&rdquo; <a href="https://github.com/python/mypy#mypyc-and-compiled-mypy" target="_blank" rel="noopener">mypy</a>: &ldquo;Mypy is itself compiled using mypyc&rdquo; (Python compiled to C extensions) and installs via <code>pip</code> (needs Python). ty, Pyrefly, zuban and Basilisk are compiled binaries, so no language runtime is required to run them.</li>
